4. EXTRAPOLATION (NOT A MEASUREMENT). Assumption G: from 65,536 updates on, each further doubling adds r times the previous doubling's increment,
   r = d(65,536)/d(32,768) of that curve (constant). Assumption S (slower decay): r_S = sqrt(r) (the decay itself relaxes). Assumption C: constant increment (no decay).
   Shown per draw (mean of panels A and B): projected i at 131,072 / 262,144 / 524,288 / 1,048,576; the first doubling U at which d(U) <= 0.10; i there; the asymptote under G.
   Three post-onset doublings carry these fits. A learning curve can decay faster (a hard storage limit) or slower (a long tail) than either assumption.
